## 4. Verify

```
gofmt -l promql/parser
go vet ./promql/parser/
go test ./promql/parser/ ./promql/ ./promql/promqltest/
```

## 5. Commit and PR

Make one commit with `git commit -s`.

- **PR title:** `promql/parser: preserve parentheses around duration literals in String()`
- **PR body:** include `Fixes #<issue>` and this release-notes block:

````
```release-notes
[BUGFIX] PromQL: Preserve parentheses around a single duration literal (e.g. `foo offset (5m)`, `foo[(5s)]`) when printing expressions with experimental duration expressions enabled.
```
````

I found the cause and wrote the plan below. Nothing has been edited and no tests have been run yet.

**Root cause:** `refactoring.FindImportStatements` (`internal/refactoring/import_statement.go`) stores import blocks in an `addrs.Map` keyed by `ConfigResource`. The check `existingResource.AbsToResource.Equal(toAddr)` is always true because the value's address equals its key. So any later block aimed at the same resource is skipped, even when it targets a different instance (`t["a"]` vs `t["b"]`) in the same module. Only the first block reaches `Context.findImportTargets`. The downstream graph and plan code (`transform_config.go`, `expandResourceImports`) already handles several targets per resource, so this is the only fix needed.

What has to keep working: an import block in a parent module overrides a child module's block for the same resource. `TestContextApply_importBlockDuplication` (or whichever test uses the `import-block-duplication` fixture, `context_apply_import_test.go:186`) covers this. Only an ancestor can target the same `toAddr`, and ancestors are visited first (pre-order walk), so "skip if the resource is already claimed by a *different* module" keeps that rule. True duplicates of the same instance in one module are already rejected by `internal/configs/module.go:528`.

## Change 1: `internal/refactoring/import_statement.go`

Replace `FindImportStatements` and `findImportStatements` (keep the `ImportStatement` type unchanged) with:

```go
// FindImportStatements recurses through the modules of the given configuration
// and returns all "import" blocks defined within.
//
// Multiple import blocks in the same module may target the same configuration
// resource (e.g. different instances of a for_each resource), and all of them
// are returned. An "import" block in a parent module overrides any import
// blocks in a child module that target the same configuration resource.
func FindImportStatements(rootCfg *configs.Config) []ImportStatement {
	return findImportStatements(rootCfg, addrs.MakeMap[addrs.ConfigResource, addrs.Module](), nil)
}

// owners records which module's import blocks claimed each config resource.
func findImportStatements(cfg *configs.Config, owners addrs.Map[addrs.ConfigResource, addrs.Module], into []ImportStatement) []ImportStatement {
	for _, mi := range cfg.Module.Import {
		// First, stitch together the module path and the RelSubject to form
		// the absolute address of the config resource being imported.
		res := mi.ToResource
		toAddr := addrs.ConfigResource{
			Module:   append(slices.Clip(cfg.Path), res.Module...),
			Resource: res.Resource,
		}

		// If an ancestor module already has import blocks for this
		// ConfigResource, they override the blocks in this module. Parents are
		// always visited before children. Import blocks from the same module
		// targeting different instances of the resource are all kept;
		// duplicate instance targets in one module are a config error.
		if owner, ok := owners.GetOk(toAddr); ok && !owner.Equal(cfg.Path) {
			continue
		}
		owners.Put(toAddr, cfg.Path)

		into = append(into, ImportStatement{
			AbsToResource:    toAddr,
			ContainingModule: cfg.Path,
			Import:           mi,
		})
	}

	for _, childCfg := range cfg.Children {
		into = findImportStatements(childCfg, owners, into)
	}

	return into
}
```

Add `"slices"` to the imports. `slices.Clip` stops toAddrs from sharing `cfg.Path`'s backing array now that several statements are kept.

## Change 2: `internal/terraform/context_plan.go`, `findImportTargets` (~line 750)

The return value is now a slice. Change the loop to:

```go
	for _, ic := range refactoring.FindImportStatements(config) {
```

Remove the `importStatements :=` line; the loop body stays the same. This is the only caller (confirmed by grep).

## Test: `internal/terraform/context_plan_import_test.go`

Add `TestContext2Plan_importForEachMultipleBlocks` right after `TestContext2Plan_importForEach` (~line 1353). Copy that test's provider and mocks setup exactly (`simpleMockProvider`, `ReadResourceResponse`, `ImportResourceStateResponse`, `ctx.Validate`, `ctx.Plan`), with this config:

```hcl
resource "test_object" "a" {
  for_each    = toset(["first", "second"])
  test_string = "foo"
}

import {
  to = test_object.a["first"]
  id = "first_id"
}

import {
  to = test_object.a["second"]
  id = "second_id"
}
```

Assertions:
- `len(plan.Changes.Resources) == 2`; otherwise fail.
- For each change, `instPlan.Importing` must be non-nil (`t.Fatalf("%s: expected import", addr)`; this is what currently fails for `["second"]`).
- `["first"]` has `Importing.ID == "first_id"` and `["second"]` has `Importing.ID == "second_id"`.
- Every change has `Action == plans.NoOp`; any other address is an error.

Optionally add a second subtest or function, `TestContext2Plan_importForEachMultipleBlocksInModule`, with the same resource and both import blocks inside a child module (`"mod/main.tf"`, `module "child" { source = "./mod" }` in root), expecting `module.child.test_object.a["first"]` and `["second"]` imported.

## Verify

```
go build ./...
go test ./internal/refactoring/...
go test ./internal/terraform/ -run 'TestContext2Plan_import|TestContextApply_import|TestContext2Validate' -count=1
go test ./internal/terraform/ ./internal/command/... -count=1
```

The new test should fail before the fix (`["second"]` has nil `Importing` / action Create) and pass after. The `import-block-duplication` apply test must still pass, which shows parent-over-child still works.

